---

NomadFS splits a file into pieces of about 256 KB, names each piece by its
SHA-256 hash, and keeps a small index of the pieces. Every piece that arrives
from another device is checked against its name, so it doesn't matter which
device sent it: any copy will do.

That one fact is what the rest builds on. It lets NomadFS keep three questions
apart, each answered by its own layer.

# [Three layers]($heading.id('layers'))

| Layer | Answers | For example |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Folders** | *What* you need | "the next 8 MB of this video", "this photo must be kept on this device", "this file was added by the phone" |
| **Distribution** | *Where from*, and *who keeps copies* | "the home server is fastest right now", "this file lives on the phone and the server" |
| **Transport** | *How* the bytes travel | encrypted connections between your devices, finding each other, relaying when a device is behind a router |

Shared folders, the swarm folder, backups, the Linux mount and the app all sit on
the top layer, and they all go through the same distribution layer. None of them
fetches pieces or makes copies on its own. So an improvement there, such as
reading from several devices at once, reaches every feature together.

# [Opening a file]($heading.id('reading'))

When you open a photo or play a video from the swarm folder:

1. **Folders** knows the file and who added it, and works out which pieces cover
   what the player asks for next. While you watch, it asks for the next few
   seconds ahead of time.
2. **Distribution** checks who holds the file: the device that added it, plus
   any backup devices and placed replicas. It then picks, for each piece, the
   device expected to answer first: `cost = (in_flight + 1) / rate`.
   With several devices holding the file, pieces come from all of them at once,
   most from the fastest. A device that turns out not to have the file is
   remembered, so it isn't asked again for every piece.
3. **Transport** carries each request over encrypted Yamux streams and checks
   each piece's SHA-256 hash before keeping it.

[Pulling a file from multiple devices in parallel: read-ahead workers spread block requests across the home server and the phone in proportion to their speed.](<$image.siteAsset('learn/multi-holder-fetch.svg').alt('Pulling a file from multiple holders in parallel: read-ahead workers spread block requests across the home server and the phone in proportion to their speed')>)

For example, when a laptop reads a photo added by a phone and backed up to a
home server, both devices hold the data. The laptop's 6 read-ahead workers
request blocks in parallel: the home server on fast LAN answers with high
throughput and takes the bulk of the chunks, while the phone on Wi-Fi serves
chunks concurrently. If a device is slow or drops off, requests automatically
shift to the other holders without interrupting the read.

A [shared folder]($link.page('docs/shared-folders')) works the same way: its
pieces come from the device sharing it, or from a backup device that keeps a copy
when that device is off.

# [Keeping copies]($heading.id('copies'))

Some devices keep copies of files that aren't theirs:

- **Backup devices** keep every file of the swarm folder, and the shared folders
  members marked for backup (a phone's camera).
- **"Keep each swarm file on N devices"** (**Node & trust → Keep each swarm file on**, or
  `--copies N`) is one setting for the whole swarm. Each file lives on the device
  that added it plus N−1 others, chosen per file among the devices that accept
  copies. Phones don't accept them unless you turn it on (**Accept copies of
  other devices' files**).

Every device works out on its own which devices keep each file, and they all
get the same answer, so no device has to coordinate the others. When a device
leaves the swarm, its files move to the next ones in line.

Nothing is pushed when you save a file. The devices that should keep it notice
it in the folder's listing and fetch it, a few pieces at a time from whoever has
them.

# [What it costs your network]($heading.id('traffic'))

NomadFS tries not to talk when it has nothing to say:

- Devices compare a short fingerprint of the swarm folder's listing every 10
  seconds, and send the listing only when it differs. A quiet swarm costs a few
  tens of kilobytes a minute per device.
- A setting such as the number of copies goes to each device once per change.
- Pieces travel only when a file is opened or must be kept. Reading a file moves
  about 1 % more than the file itself.

The [learning book](https://github.com/highercomve/nomadfs/tree/main/learning)
explains each layer in detail, with the code: start with
[the distribution layer](https://github.com/highercomve/nomadfs/blob/main/learning/cluster/overview.md).
